27.1.m:
"Purify thyself for the lord of speech!"—
"Purify thyself for lordship over speech!" is indeed what he says by this.
Therefore by this [speech] we speak at will and we breathe forth.
Prajāpati indeed was this [universe];
speech was his second.
He entered into pairing with her;
she conceived a child;
she stepped away from him;
she created these offspring;
she entered back into Prajāpati alone.
"Purify thyself for the lord of speech!"—
from this very pair he is propagated with offspring and with cattle.
"Purify thyself for the lord of speech!"—
sacred prayer is indeed the lord of speech;
by sacred prayer alone he purifies it.
"By the shoots of the bull, purified by the hands!"—
Indra is indeed the bull; his hands are the rays [or hands];
by them alone he purifies it.
"Thou art the divine strainer of the gods!"—
to the deities alone he assigns it.
"Our honeyed refreshments!"—
for with Soma they purify Soma.
"Whose portion thou art!"—
to the deities alone he assigns it.
"Make our refreshments honeyed!"—
the refreshments are indeed the deities;
for the deities alone he makes it honeyed.
"Thou art self-prepared!"—
he makes breath his own.
"Go through the wide intermediate space!"—
for it has the intermediate space as its deity at that time.
"For all the senses!"—
he places breath in both the gods and men.
"Mind hath prepared thee!"—
for breath is held by mind.
"Hail to thee, the well-formed one, for the sun!"—
Soma is indeed the well-formed one;
in yonder sun are the dear bodies of the gods;
those alone he gratifies.
Moreover, by the utterance of "svāhā" alone he prepares the offerings in it.
He offers it as completely offered;
therefore breath goes outward.
He offers standing upright;
the sacrifice is co-measured with man;
man is continuous through in-breath and out-breath;
for the non-shattering of in-breath and out-breath.
The Adhvaryu does not stumble who knows thus.
He should offer the Upāṃśu from the north, the Antaryāma from the south.
A chariot of the gods indeed is this sacrifice;
its two reins are these: the Upāṃśu and the Antaryāma.
He arranges the reins in front of the divine chariot
for the arrangement of the year, for the discernment of the heavenly world.
In imitation of that, the two reins of a human chariot are arranged.
They are to be offered straight,
for the non-intertwining of the breaths.
He should wipe upwards if he should desire, "May it not rain!";
yonder alone he holds back the rain.
He should wipe downwards if he should desire, "May it rain!";
the offering that rises up from here causes rain to fall from yonder;
with the Soma-offering alone he leads down rain from heaven.
"To the ray-drinking gods thee!"—so he wipes [it] on the enclosing stick;
the ray-drinking gods are indeed the breaths;
those alone he gratifies.
Moreover, this is the Soma-drink of the enclosing sticks.
He wipes on the inside;
therefore the breaths are inside.
He cuts off the sacrifice when he brings it to completion before the pressing.
When he unites the unpressed shoots,
[it is] for the continuity of the sacrifice, for non-interruption.
He unites six shoots;
therefore there are six breaths.
In pairs [he unites them];
therefore the breaths are in pairs.
Being moist, they do not cling together;
therefore the breaths, being moist, do not cling together.
Whoever indeed knows the Soma-drink of Soma is not cut off from the Soma-drink, nor his offspring.
"O Soma, what inviolable, wakeful name is thine, to that Soma, to thee, hail!"—
this indeed is the Soma-drink of Soma;
he thereby gratifies Soma with Soma;
he who knows thus is not cut off from the Soma-drink, nor his offspring.
If he should practice sorcery, he should take [it] with: "On the impulse of the divine Savitṛ, with the arms of the Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan I take thee!";
"In the exhilaration of the exhilarating, intoxicating [drink], may it be false, then I will offer thee!"—
he makes it false indeed.
Soma belongs to all deities;
all the deities at that time hope in this one: "He will offer to me, he will offer to me!";
the deities, desiring the oblation, burn him up.
He stands until he is breathless;
he reaches the very end of his breath.
When he speaks the yajus,
he cuts him off from the gods.
He practices sorcery with the Upāṃśu;
the Upāṃśu is breath;
with breath alone he practices sorcery against his breath;
quickly he perishes.

28.1.m:
The Dhruva [cup] is life.
It is drawn last;
therefore life is the last among the vital breaths.
It is drawn ninth;
for there are nine vital breaths.
He sets it down solitary in the northern Havirdhāna [cart];
therefore the downward breath, alone among the vital breaths, has half a share.
After the Dhruva, indeed, human beings are propagated; after the other cups, the other creatures.
He sets down the Dhruva on [an un-earthed place] where earth is not strewn; the other cups on where earth is strewn;
therefore man stands firm with flesh, animals with bone.
A prince guards [it];
a noble is indeed the lord of creatures; the Dhruva is life;
he guards life itself for the creatures;
and also he guards royal power.
If he should desire: 'May they expel him who is settled, may he who is expelled become settled,' [saying:] 'Thee, the un-firm, possessing an un-firm dwelling, un-firmest of the un-firm, most immovable of the immovable, I push out from such-and-such a folk,' he should push out the Dhruva.
Firm indeed is he who is settled; un-firm is he who is expelled.
He expels him who is settled; he causes him who is expelled to become settled; quickly they change places.
If he were to do so, he would dislodge the life of creatures.
He should pass grass over and above with this mantra;
thereby he causes the expelled to become settled, [and] does not dislodge the life of creatures.
The altar is indeed the realm of the gods;
as great as is the altar, so great is the earth;
by means of the realm of the gods he drives him away from the realm of men.
He should draw [it] full for him of whom he should desire: 'May he go to the full span of life.'
He draws full life for him;
he goes to the full span of life.
He should draw a little for him of whom he should desire: 'May he go to a short span of life.'
He makes his life short; quickly he perishes.
He should draw above the half for him of whom he should desire: 'May he go to higher life.'
He brings him to higher life.
According to the characteristic form he grasps life for him.
He draws [it] enclosed on both sides with Vaiśvānara [verses];
therefore the vital breaths are enclosed on both sides with Vaiśvānara.
[With the verse:] 'Head of heaven, the messenger of the earth,'
he places life both in the gods and in men.
The sacrifice is commensurate with man; man is continuous with life.
When this [cup], drawn in the morning, lies until the third pressing,
by means of life he makes the sacrifice continuous.
In the morning indeed these two are drawn, in the world of the Gāyatrī: the Āgrāyaṇa and the Dhruva.
They two lie until the third pressing from the world of the Jagatī.
Therefore the Gāyatrī, not having lost its potency, returns at the later pressing;
that is its allotted portion.
The Gāyatrī is breath; the Dhruva is life.
Because this, placed in the morning, lies until the third pressing,
therefore as great as is life, so great is breath.
The Dhruva is life, Agni is Rudra;
it should not be offered alone;
he would burn the life of creatures; they would be liable to die.
He pours [it] into the Hotṛ's cup.
The Hotṛ belongs to All the Gods; these creatures belong to All the Gods;
he places life in the creatures themselves.
Indeed, he makes it un-firm when he pours [it] out;
consequent upon its un-firmness, things do not succeed for the creatures.
[Saying:] 'May the divine clans be strengthened, may men prosper, may the heavenly things be in heaven, the birds in the intermediate space, the earthly things on earth,' he pours out the Dhruva.
According to their proper place he strengthens the creatures.
When summoned, he should pour it out at the Uktha;
by means of what has been chanted he continues the recitation.
If he should pour it out when summoned, he would make the Uktha redundant;
if when recited, he would make the cup redundant.
At the middle concluding verse, at that he pours it out.
These are indeed the limbs of the sacrifice, namely the Ukthamadas; the Dhruva is life.
One half of a man's self is the limbs, one half is life;
having joined the ends on both sides, he places life in the middle.
'From what reality,' the knowers of sacred speech ask, 'do the rushing waters not dwindle, do not dry up?'
Awaiting this very Soma-drink, which is the Dhruva;
for this pushes them away.
Sitting facing west, he pours [it] out;
along the current indeed he lets them flow.
He should not urinate halfway through;
he should sit through the Agniṣṭoma.
The Dhruva is life;
he would cause life to flow away.
As great indeed is the Agniṣṭoma, so great is the sacrifice.
When he sits through the Agniṣṭoma,
he goes within his breath;
he goes to the full span of life.
The Sadas is the belly of this sacrifice;
thither they run, slipping out.
The sacrifice is Prajāpati;
when he sits through the Agniṣṭoma,
he does not empty Prajāpati's belly; he does not become hungry.

28.2.m:
The gods, having slain the Asuras and fleeing from blood-money, confused the seasons, the year.
The gods sought its ordering.
They ordered it by means of the seasonal cups.
That the seasonal cups are drawn
is for the ordering of the year,
for the discernment of the heavenly world.
Both draw together first;
for both of these proceed together to order the year.
Both [draw] together last;
having ordered the year, both are unyoked together.
The two season-vessels have mouths on both sides;
for the seasons have mouths on both sides;
for we do not know from which side is the mouth of the seasons.
They should not cross each other.
If they should cross each other, season would become aggressive towards season.
Because they pass each other,
therefore they say: "Like two horses, the seasons have sniffed each other."
Twelve of these are drawn;
twelve months are a year,
for the ordering of the year.
The year is the heavenly world;
these are its steps, its crossings,
that the seasonal cups [are].
He ascends an ascent by means of these,
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
Six are of one kind, six of the other.
That sun is the season;
therefore it goes north for six months, south for six.
Six with "Through the season";
six indeed are the seasons;
he firmly establishes himself in the seasons.
Four with "Through the seasons";
four-footed are cattle;
cattle are propagated along the year;
from the year he propagates cattle for him.
Twice again with "Through the season";
two-footed is the sacrificer;
he firmly establishes himself above the cattle.
These are the Soma-drinkers, Indra's kin among the Soma-drinkers,
that the seasonal cups [are].
For he says: "Let him drink Soma through the season,"
not "The season [shall drink]";
they do not offer to the season.
These are their own two vessels, by which they offer to them;
therefore a kinsman brings tribute in his own vessel;
they bring tribute to him who knows thus.
They drink alternating;
for freshness.
Therefore it rains season by season.
Having ordered the year by this, having discerned the heavenly world, the gods placed this light of the sacrifice above.
That the Aindrāgna [cup] is drawn,
he places light at the opening of the sacrifice,
for the ordering of the year,
for the discernment of the heavenly world.
Therefore the light traverses above and over the year.
He draws the Aindrāgna [cup] with the season-vessel;
Indra-Agni are the nobility, the seasons are the peasantry;
he inserts the nobility into the peasantry.
The All-Gods, having their share in the third pressing, desired the morning pressing.
Prajāpati saw that they were to be gratified in this cup.
That the Vaiśvadeva [cup] is drawn at the morning pressing,
he gratifies the All-Gods at the morning pressing.
Gratified, pleased, they release the two later pressings for him.
He draws the Vaiśvadeva [cup] with the Śukra-vessel.
That sun is the Śukra, these creatures belong to the All-Gods;
therefore it faces all creatures towards the west;
for they think: "Towards me, towards me!"

28.3.m:
At the midday pressing, Indra slew Vṛtra with the Maruts by virility.
The chant is related to the Maruts, the recitation is related to the Maruts, the cups are related to the Maruts;
he enriches the sacrificer with virility from every side;
he slays Vṛtra from every side.
The first Marutvatīya [cup] is the aiming [of the weapon];
the second is the drawing back;
with the third he releases the discharge.
With the first Marutvatīya [cup] he raises the thunderbolt;
with the second he hurls it;
with the third he lays [the foe] low.
For whomever he might hate, he should draw [the cups] not dedicated to the Maruts for him;
the Maruts are virility;
with virility he causes him to fail.
Indra, about to slay Vṛtra, gave a gift to the gods, the sacrificial fees;
with the Marutvatīya [cups] he slew Vṛtra.
Therefore one should not give when [the hymn] to Marutvat has been recited;
for Vṛtra has then been slain;
a victory has been won.
Nor should he accept;
he displaces established cattle, he becomes cattleless.
Indra, having slain Vṛtra, became Mahendra (the Great Indra);
he looked down on the other gods;
he took out this cup as his choice share, the Mahendrīya [cup].
Therefore a king, having won a battle, drives off his choice share.
The gods said to him: "You have said everything; let there be a share for us here too."
[He said:] "How should there be a share for you?"
"Let him sacrifice with the Virāj for this cup."
The Virāj is the gods' vessel;
that he sacrifices with the Virāj,
he gratifies the deities with the gods' vessel.
Therefore when a king wins a battle, [a share is given] there even to the charioteers and village heads.
The Trivṛt [stoma] has its domain through the Gāyatrī, the Pañcadaśa through the Triṣṭubh, the Ekavim̐śa through the Jagatī.
Now this Virāj
is the very domain where the Saptadaśa [is].
That he sacrifices with the Virāj,
he makes the Saptadaśa possess a domain.
For whomever he might hate, he should sacrifice with the Triṣṭubh for him;
the Triṣṭubh is force, virility;
by force and virility he cuts him off from his domain;
he, deprived of domain, perishes.
The gods, having slain the Asuras and fleeing from blood-money, entered the sacrifice from the middle.
They entered this verse, the Virāj.
That he sacrifices with the Virāj
is for the propitiation of the deities.
The Ādityas, going to the heavenly world, pushed the Aṅgirases back into this world together with the sacrifice.
Those Aṅgirases said to the Ādityas: "For how many of you shall we convey the oblation?"
"For thirty-three," they said:
"ten Vasus, Indra the eleventh;
ten Rudras, Indra the eleventh;
ten Ādityas, Indra the eleventh;
these are thirty deities, thirty;
the vaṣaṭ-call is of two syllables;
speech is of one syllable;"
thereby it belongs to Indra.
His deities are accommodated in the oblation;
his oblation goes according to the deity.
They choose him for the office of priest who knows thus;
for as many as the deities are, so many he causes the oblation to reach.

28.4.m:
From the midday pressing, indeed, the gods went to the heavenly world.
The sacrificial gifts are their steps and bridge-crossings.
When he gives sacrificial gifts,
having constructed a bridge, he goes to the heavenly world.
As many living creatures as he gives here, so many are the vital breaths in yonder world;
therefore much is to be given.
Therefore here they praise what is given generously;
he constructs a greater bridge.
At this time indeed the deities bend down closest [to the earth];
therefore at this time he [the sun] burns most intensely, since he climbs to the heavenly world from the closest distance.
He offers with two verses to Sūrya,
for the attainment of the heavenly world,
with two [verses];
the sacrificer is two-footed;
he makes him step upon this [earth].
When he offers in the Āgnīdhra [fire] with a verse to Agni,
he makes him step in the intermediate space.
When he gives,
he makes him reach the heavenly world.
‘Go to the sky, go to the light,’ [he says],
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
‘I have approached your form with form, [your] age with age,’ [he says];
this indeed is the form of cattle, namely ghee, namely gold;
with form indeed he approaches their form, with age, age.
‘May Tutha Viśvavedas distribute you,’ [he says];
Tutha Viśvavedas is indeed truth;
with truth, with the brahman, he distributes them.
‘This, O Agni, is your gift that comes, brought by Soma,’ [he says];
cattle are connected with Agni;
they are given in the Soma [sacrifice];
‘Lead it by the path of Mitra,’ [he says];
by the path of Mitra indeed he leads them.
He leads [them] between the Āgnīdhra [shed] and the Sadas [shed];
by this path indeed the gods went to the heavenly world;
he causes them to reach that very [path].
From the south, indeed, the Rakṣases conquered the sacrifice of the gods;
they drove them away again from the Āgnīdhra [fire].
When they lead the sacrificial gifts south from the Āgnīdhra,
[it is] for the winning of the sacrifice,
for the smiting away of the Rakṣases.
He offers in the Āgnīdhra [fire] with a verse to Agni;
cattle are connected with Agni,
for the redemption of the cattle.
He should offer with a second verse to Varuṇa if he is about to give a horse, or a cart, or a chariot;
for horses belong to Varuṇa, trees belong to Varuṇa;
Agni is all the deities,
for the propitiation of the deities.
He gives a cushion;
this is indeed the form of the metres;
by means of the form indeed he secures the metres.
He gives gold;
endowed with golden radiance, he makes them go to the heavenly world.
‘Given by us, full of sweetness, go to the gods,’ [he says];
he makes them go to the heavenly world.
‘Enter into the giver,’ [he says];
therefore in yonder world they are prone to return [to him].
‘Go forth by the path of ṛta, O sacrificial gifts of silver!’ [he says];
that which is worthy of sacrifice, fit for offering, that is the path of ṛta;
by that indeed he leads them.
‘May I obtain a brāhmaṇa, prosperous, having [distinguished] fathers, having a patrilineage,’ [he says];
he who is learned in the Veda and of ṛṣi descent, he has [distinguished] fathers and a patrilineage;
‘May I find him,’ thus in truth he says this;
for he is one to whom sacrificial gifts are well placed.
That [gift] which he gives to a non-brāhmaṇa spreads out as trees;
that which he gives to the Kaṇvas and Kaśyapas spreads out as fog;
that which he gives to a brāhmaṇa who is not learned in the Veda spreads out as mirages;
that which he gives to one learned in the Veda and of ṛṣi descent enriches him in yonder world, and in this world it serves for his enjoyment again;
therefore he alone is to be sought.
‘Look abroad at the light, look abroad at the intermediate space,’ [he says, and] looks at the Sadas.
Soma-drinkers indeed are these brāhmaṇas;
their eyes are full of fiery energy.
They are hostile to the giver;
they are capable of repelling him;
he pays homage to them,
to prevent repelling.
He gives the gold of Atri.
Svarbhānu the Asura pierced the sun with darkness;
Atri was the first to find him.
That he gives the gold of Atri
is for the attainment of the heavenly world.
If he should not find a descendant of Atri, he should give it to one who is of ṛṣi descent and united [in study];
thereby indeed he prospers.
The Asuras indeed led sacrificial gifts to the gods;
they repelled them;
they said, ‘This one has not been affectionate.’
They led another to them;
they repelled it;
they said, ‘This one has gone to manly valor.’
They led another to them;
they repelled it;
they said, ‘This one has run away.’
Concerning these, indeed, the ṛṣi recited:
‘The black drop stood down upon the Aṃśumatī, speeding with ten thousands; / Indra aided him who was blowing with might; he held back the slaughtering, insolent bands.’
She, having become a she-hyena, entered the Asuras;
she burned them out.
Therefore a sacrificial gift that has been repelled must not be accepted;
he must not let it mingle among the cattle.
Having become a she-hyena, she enters him;
she burns him out.

28.6.m:
The Ādityas, having slain the Asuras and fleeing from blood-money, entered the gods.
They entered the [gods] with two deities.
For these are the most united among the gods;
therefore the Āditya [cup] is drawn from the [cups] with two deities.
The Ādityas indeed were born from the remnant [of the oblation];
therefore this remnant Soma is drawn.
The Ādityas indeed have the remnant as their share;
therefore this remnant Soma is drawn.
He draws [it] behind an enclosure.
Agni indeed is Rudra; the Āditya [cup] is livestock,
for the protection of livestock.
The gods indeed excluded Rudra from the sacrifice.
They assigned together: “This is your share in the mother”; bringing it thus far to the end of the Āditya [cup], in connection with this they shut [him] out from the sacrifice.
When he draws the Āditya [cup] behind an enclosure,
he shuts Rudra out from the sacrifice.
Livestock indeed are the Ādityas.
Since this Soma is drawn abundantly,
therefore livestock, being eaten, are not exhausted.
When there are many inside the altar, then he should draw [it];
he secures them as proper food.
When his rival is inside the altar, then he should draw [it];
this indeed is the sacrificer's own world, namely the altar;
livestock are the Ādityas;
in his own world he strips off the livestock of the rival.
The introductory verses are Bṛhatī verses;
livestock indeed are the Bṛhatīs;
livestock is the Āditya [cup];
he places livestock in livestock.
He draws Soma first, then curds, then Soma.
Curds indeed are nourishment, the Āditya [cup] is livestock;
he places nourishment right in the middle among livestock.
It has boiled milk as coagulant;
therefore the raw [cow] yields the cooked [milk].
If he should desire: “May it rain,” he should mix [it] from above with curds, saying: “With that heavenly rain I mix you.”
Curds indeed are livestock, the Āditya [cup] is livestock;
it rains for livestock indeed whenever it rains;
he brings down rain for livestock.
He stirs it with the Upāṃśusavana stone;
breath indeed is the Upāṃśusavana stone, the Āditya [cup] is livestock;
he places breath in livestock.
“O Vivasvat, O Āditya, this is your Soma-drink!”
That Upāṃśusavana stone is indeed he;
he enriches him directly with the Soma-drink.
If a drop falls down from what is lifted up, it rains immediately.
Having drawn it, he covers it,
for the holding of embryos, against falling down.
He moves it forward at the tenth chant;
therefore embryos, being deposited, are born in the tenth month.
Looking elsewhere, he offers;
Agni indeed is Rudra, the Āditya [cup] is livestock,
for the protection of livestock.
If he were to look, a supervising brahmin might seize the livestock;
he becomes a non-superviser of the livestock being given away.

28.7.m:
With the Antaryāma vessel he draws the Sāvitra [cup].
Livestock indeed is the Antaryāma [cup];
impelled by Savitṛ, livestock are born;
impelled by Savitṛ he generates them.
He offers it unset;
therefore livestock are born in the field unset.
The gods indeed could not lift the third pressing.
At that time Savitṛ was at the morning pressing.
The gods said to him: “Let us lift this with you.”
“No,” he said, “I am the chief; I will not retreat backward.”
“Be the chief here!” they said, “Be the chief here, and then let us lift [it] with you.”
They lifted it with Savitṛ.
That the Sāvitra [cup] is drawn
is for the lifting of the third pressing.
With “Unto thee, divine Savitṛ,” he is chief at the morning pressing.
Savitṛ indeed encompassed that sacrifice all around;
he who knows thus encompasses the rival all around.
With the Antaryāma vessel he draws the Sāvitra [cup] from the Āgrāyaṇa [cup] at the third pressing.
In the morning indeed this is drawn in the domain of the Gāyatrī, namely the Āgrāyaṇa;
he distributes it at the third pressing in the domain of the Jagatī;
therefore the Sāvitra [cup] is drawn from the Āgrāyaṇa;
for Savitṛ among the gods belongs to the Gāyatrī.
Upon the residue of the Sāvitra [cup] he draws supplementary [Soma] for the All-gods.
This is indeed the same cup,
for continuity.
These offspring belong to the All-gods;
impelled by Savitṛ, offspring are born;
impelled by Savitṛ he generates them.
“Thou art good refuge, having a good resting place,” [he says];
for he draws supplementary Soma upon Soma.
And he makes the upper a good refuge for the lower, and the lower a good resting place for the upper.
“Homage to the Bṛhadukṣa,” [he says];
for Bṛhat is of the gods, homage of the fathers.
The fathers indeed have recitation as their share, not chant as their share;
wherever he recites the recitation for them, there he draws the cup for them.

28.9.m:
Indra indeed slew Vṛtra.
He broke open his head;
that became the droṇakalaśa trough.
The Soma that flowed together from that, that became the Hāriyojana [cup].
Indra indeed, having slain Vṛtra, poured together the Soma from his lung and heart;
that became the Hāriyojana [cup].
He thought: “If I offer this unboiled and without a second, the Asuras will arise after [me]; if I do not offer it, they will arise after [me].”
The Soma was finished; the enclosing-sticks had been thrown [into the fire].
Then he arose to offer.
Agni said to him: “You shall not offer this unboiled and without a second in me.”
Into it he threw parched grains;
he offered it boiled and having a second.
That he throws in parched grains,
is just for becoming boiled, for having a second.
Those cattle which were in Vṛtra, these are they;
he thus secures livestock that yield desires;
for he fulfills every desire of these.
The Ṛgveda and Sāmaveda are indeed the two bay steeds of Indra;
this is the portion of the two of them, namely the Hāriyojana [cup].
The enclosing-sticks are the stalls;
that he offers the Hāriyojana [cup] when the enclosing-sticks have been thrown [into the fire],
he covers the fodder for the two without a stall.
Prajāpati indeed is the Āgrāyaṇa [cup];
from the Āgrāyaṇa this Soma is left over.
Whatever is left over of the sacrifice, that is left over for Prajāpati.
That he offers this surplus Soma,
therefore this Prajāpati is surplus;
he purifies towards creatures.
With the droṇakalaśa trough the Unnetṛ offers the Hāriyojana [cup].
Surplus indeed is this among vessels, namely the droṇakalaśa trough;
surplus is this [priest] among priests, namely the Unnetṛ;
surplus is this [Soma] among Somas, namely the Hāriyojana [cup];
by means of the surplus he obtains the surplus.
They ask for an invitation from the Unnetṛ,
for the securing of whatever Soma-drink is there.
Those cattle which were in Vṛtra, these are they.
If he were to crush [the parched grains], destructive livestock, few in number, would attend upon him, consuming [him],
for he hurts [them].
If he were not to crush [them], non-destructive livestock, many in number, would attend upon him without consuming [him],
for he does not hurt [them].
He should squeeze [them] as it were;
he does both;
non-destructive livestock, many in number, attend upon him, consuming [for him].
He should suck out as it were,
for the securing of whatever Soma-drink is there.
He scatters [them] on the high altar;
the high altar is livestock; the Hāriyojana grains are livestock;
he establishes livestock in livestock.

29.10.m:
The one for Agni, the one for Sarasvatī, the one for Soma, the one for Bṛhaspati: one should seize these together if desiring spiritual lustre, desiring offspring, desiring cattle, desiring the office of purohita, desiring the office of purohita.
In that there is one for Agni,
for the brāhmaṇa belongs to Agni.
In that there is one for Sarasvatī,
for Sarasvatī is speech,
for he speaks speech.
In that there is one for Soma,
for he drinks Soma.
In that there is one for Bṛhaspati,
for Bṛhaspati is the brāhmaṇa;
having made the brāhmaṇa four-footed, he establishes it;
he becomes endowed with spiritual lustre.
Then the one for Pūṣan:
Pūṣan is cattle;
he yokes cattle after the brāhmaṇa.
Then the one for Indra and Agni,
then the one for Indra:
Indra and Agni are royal power, Indra is royal power;
having brought royal power to completion, he establishes it;
and with royal power he pushes cattle toward the brāhmaṇa.
The one for Agni is in the middle, those for Indra on both sides.
The brāhmaṇa belongs to Agni, the rājanya to Indra;
with the brāhmaṇa, royal power glides apart in the middle.
He attains the office of purohita who knows thus.
He makes Indra and Agni the greatest share-holders of the cattle.
Agni's is that for Agni, Indra's is that for Indra, that for Indra and Agni is for both together;
therefore the brāhmaṇa and the rājanya are the greatest share-holders among creatures.
Then the one for the Viśve Devas,
then the one for the Maruts:
the Viśve Devas are the commonalty, the Maruts are the commonalty;
having brought the commonalty to completion, he yokes it after royal power;
having made royal power head the brāhmaṇa, he yokes the commonalty after it.
The one for Savitṛ is for impulsion;
the one for Varuṇa is for freedom from Varuṇa.
Properly he unites the brāhmaṇa, properly royal power, properly the commonalty.
Where the properly arranged set of eleven is seized, there it is in order for creatures:
the brāhmaṇa becomes a brāhmaṇa, the rājanya a rājanya, the vaiśya a vaiśya.
Following this disordered [arrangement] of the set of eleven, indeed, it is not in order for creatures.
This indeed is the set of eleven;
this the Kāpeyas know;
this he should seize as the final one of the Atirātra.
This other is sacrificial-session-like, which creatures follow falling into trouble.
This is the division of days;
these days have these deities.
Thus ends the twenty-ninth sthānaka, named Dīrghajihvī, in the Madhyamikā of the Caraka recension in the glorious Kāṭhaka Yajurveda.

30.3.m:
One who desires, "May it be arranged for offspring according to prior order," should draw [cups] with Aindravāyava at the head.
Following the proper arrangement of the sacrifice, it is arranged for offspring;
following an improper arrangement of the sacrifice, it is not arranged.
He arranges for offspring according to prior order;
the younger does not surpass the older.
For one who is ill, he should draw [cups] with Aindravāyava at the head.
He whose [body] is ill is deprived of breath;
the Aindravāyava is breath;
he prospers him with breath itself.
Those among whose consecrated ones one dies should draw [cups] with Maitrāvaruṇa at the head.
Those among whose consecrated ones one dies are deprived of out-breath and in-breath;
Mitrā-Varuṇā are out-breath and in-breath;
they encompass out-breath and in-breath at the mouth;
therefore out-breath and in-breath are at the mouth.
A late-born [son] should draw [cups] with Āśvina at the head.
The Aśvins were late-born among the gods;
they attained the top;
the Aśvins are his deities, who is late-born;
he takes hold of them;
they lead him to the top.
One who has attained prosperity should draw [cups] with Śukra at the head.
Yonder sun is Śukra;
this is the end;
having reached the end of prosperity, a man turns back;
from the end he seizes the end;
he does not become worse than that, such as he is when sacrificing.
One practicing sorcery should draw [cups] with Manthin at the head.
An afflicted vessel is this vessel of Manthin;
he causes him to be seized by death itself.
One against whom sorcery is practiced should draw [cups] with Ukthya at the head.
The sensory power and virility of all vessels is the vessel of Ukthya;
with all sensory power he overpowers him.
"May we not go from thee to desolate fields; do not kick away [the pail] with milk; do not burn us! / May we not suffer for sin committed by another; / O Sarasvatī, lead us to better [fortune]!"
so [he says].
Desolate fields belong to death;
thereby he does not go to the fields of death.
One whose father and grandfather were virtuous, yet he does not attain that, should draw [cups] with Āgrāyaṇa at the head.
He whose father and grandfather are virtuous, yet he does not attain that, is deprived of speech and sensory power.
The breast of the sacrifice, as it were, speech as it were, is the Āgrāyaṇa;
he prospers him with speech, with sensory power.
For one who is ill, he should draw full cups.
Pain reaches the breaths of him who is ill;
the cups are breaths;
he frees his breaths from pain.
When Parjanya does not rain, he should draw full cups.
Pain reaches the breaths of creatures when Parjanya does not rain;
the cups are breaths;
he frees the breaths of creatures from pain;
it rains suddenly.

30.4.m:
"O Lord of the field, yield to us a sweet wave like a milk-cow milk, / dripping with honey, like clarified butter well-purified; may the lords of truth be gracious to us!"
"Thou art taken with a support; / for Aditi of four udders, thee!"
"Stir up from the ocean, O Maruts; do ye, rich in moisture, cause rain to fall! / Your milk-cows, O wondrous ones, fail not; the chariots have turned in pursuit of those who journey in beauty."
"Thou art taken with a support; / for Aditi of four udders, thee!"
"The great mother of those of good vows, the wife of truth, we would invoke for aid, / of mighty rule, unaging, widespread, affording good shelter, Aditi of good guidance."
"Thou art taken with a support; / for Aditi of four udders, thee!"
"This is thy womb; / for Aditi of four udders, thee!"
Rain departed from the gods;
it settled in these worlds;
the gods resolved to seek it;
they saw this cup;
they drew it;
thereby they brought down rain from these worlds.
One who desires rain should draw this [cup]:
"O Lord of the field, a sweet wave..." [he says];
yonder Lord of the field rains from up there;
"Stir up from the ocean, O Maruts..." [he says];
the Maruts bring the released rain;
"The great mother of those of good vows..." [he says];
this [earth] is Aditi; on her it rains;
these deities rule over rain;
them he has recourse to;
he brings down rain from these worlds.
The vessel has four teats;
four are the quarters;
from the quarters he brings down rain.
Whichever teat it first enters, from that quarter it rains.
Even while the vessel is being made, it rains.
With an earthen [vessel] he draws;
with a wooden one he offers;
for an earthen [vessel] has not attained the oblation.
